  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nA sprawling mid/upper-level anticyclone over the Great Basin and \nFour Corners is amplifying and expanding eastward across the Rockies \ninto the central and southern Plains through the weekend. This \npattern favors warming and sporadic isolated storm chances across \nKansas.\n\nFor this afternoon and tonight, a few weak boundaries appear in \nobservations. The first is expected to remain south of the area, \nwhile the other is to our north across southern Nebraska. The broader\nenvironment remains warm and moist, but with significant capping in \nplace, the likelihood of storms developing around our area is \nminimal. An MCS may develop in central Nebraska and push southeast, \npotentially clipping our far northeast late tonight into tomorrow \nmorning as the low-level jet increases, but the latest model runs \nhave grown increasingly pessimistic in this scenario playing out. \nGenerally, a hot and dry forecast is favored through early next week \nas the core of the upper ridge shifts farther east over the \ncentral/southern Plains, reinforcing a hot air mass over much of \nKansas while a surface boundary slowly meanders nearby. \n\nWeak perturbations riding the northern periphery of the ridge and \nthe lingering boundary will periodically bring low chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms, mainly during the late afternoon and \nevening hours, particularly across north central and far northeast \nKansas closer to the frontal zone. However, stronger capping and \nsubsidence under the ridge suggest that storm coverage will be \nlimited and spatially inconsistent, with many locations remaining dry\nfor extended periods despite the presence of the boundary. Heat \nindices in the low 100s are once again likely for the area beginning \ntomorrow and lasting into next week. \n\nOverall, the forecast remains characterized by episodic, mainly\nboundary-focused storm chances against a backdrop of increasingly\noppressive heat as the ridge peaks over the central Plains.",
